Below is a “cheat‑sheet” of the most common mistakes people make when they start using C++20 **concepts**, together with concrete examples and the practical tricks that keep your code clean, fast, and future‑proof.

| # | What went wrong | Why it fails (or why it’s fragile) | How to avoid / fix | Example |
|---|-----------------|------------------------------------|--------------------|---------|
| **1** | **Concept syntax errors** – e.g. writing `concept IsIntegral = requires(T t) { t == 0; };` instead of `concept IsIntegral = requires(T t) { t == 0; };` | `requires` must be a *concept* definition, not a free statement. The compiler sees `requires` as a keyword only in the context of a concept. | Make sure you start with `concept Name = requires(...) ;` or `template<class T> concept Name = ... ;`. Always put the `=` after the name. | ```cpp
// Wrong
concept IsIntegral = requires(T t) { t == 0; }; // T is undeclared
// Right
concept IsIntegral = requires(int t) { t == 0; }; // or
template<class T> concept IsIntegral = requires(T t) { t == 0; };
``` |
| **2** | **Using `requires` in the function body instead of the signature** | A `requires` clause inside the body does **not** constrain the template. It’s just a compile‑time assertion that will trigger only when the function is called. | Put the `requires` clause on the function *declaration*, *before* the return type (or in the trailing `requires` form). | ```cpp
// Wrong
template<class T>
T foo(T x) { requires requires { return x + 1; }; return x + 1; }

// Right
// 1. Function‑parameter requires clause
template<class T> requires std::same_as<decltype(std::declval<T>() + 1), T>
T foo(T x) { return x + 1; }

// 2. Trailing requires clause
template<class T>
T foo(T x) requires std::same_as<decltype(std::declval<T>() + 1), T>
{ return x + 1; }
``` |
| **3** | **Over‑broad or over‑narrow concept definitions** | *Broad* → accepts too many types, masking bugs.<br>*Narrow* → rejects perfectly fine types. | Start small, then add requirements as needed. Keep the intent clear and use “and” (`&&`) for the minimal set of operations you care about. | ```cpp
// Too broad – accepts any type with operator+ defined (even for strings!)
concept Addable = requires(T a, T b) { a + b; };

// Better – only types that can add to themselves and produce the same type
concept Addable = requires(T a, T b) { a + b -> std::same_as<T>; };
``` |
| **4** | **Concepts that use expressions on incomplete types** | The compiler will try to instantiate the expression, which can be a hard error if the type isn’t defined yet. | Delay the concept check until the type is complete, e.g. use it only in contexts where the type is fully defined, or wrap the expression in a `requires` that first checks for a trait that is valid on incomplete types. | ```cpp
// Wrong – concept used before class definition
concept Sizeable = requires(T t) { sizeof(t); };

// Right – check with a trait that’s valid on incomplete types
concept Sizeable = requires { sizeof(std::declval<T>()); };
``` |
| **5** | **Using concepts in the *wrong* template parameter list** | `template<class T> requires C<T> struct X {};` is correct. But writing `template<C<T> T> struct X {};` is illegal. | Either use a trailing `requires` clause **or** put the concept in the *constraint* part of the template parameter list (`requires C<T>`). | ```cpp
// Wrong
template<C<T> T> struct X {};

// Right
// 1. Constraint on the template parameter list
template<class T> requires C<T> struct X {};

// 2. Trailing requires clause (C++20 style)
template<class T> struct X requires C<T> {};
``` |
| **6** | **Concepts in function overload resolution cause ambiguity** | Two overloads that differ only by a concept constraint can become ambiguous if both constraints are satisfied. | Use *substitution failure* to make the constraints exclusive, or give each overload a distinct signature. | ```cpp
// Ambiguous: both std::is_integral_v<T> and std::is_floating_point_v<T> can be true for T = double
std::vector<int> foo(int);          // unconstrained
std::vector<int> foo(float) requires std::is_integral_v<T>;

// Fix: make constraints exclusive
std::vector<int> foo(int) requires std::is_integral_v<T>;
std::vector<int> foo(float) requires std::is_floating_point_v<T>;
``` |
| **7** | **Using concepts with partial specialization incorrectly** | A constrained partial specialization cannot use a constraint that depends on a template parameter that isn’t a *deduced* parameter. | Write the constraint directly in the `requires` clause of the partial specialization, not inside the template parameter list. | ```cpp
// Wrong – constraint uses a parameter not deduced
 template<class T> requires std::same_as<T, int> struct A {}; // fine

 // For partial specialization
 template<class T, class U> struct B {};
 template<class T, class U> requires std::same_as<U, int> struct B<T, U> {}; // OK
``` |
| **8** | **Concepts that depend on non‑type template parameters (NTTPs)** | `requires requires { static_assert(sizeof(T) == N); }` fails because `N` is not a *deduced* value. | Make the constraint a separate template parameter or use a `requires` clause that takes the NTTP explicitly. | ```cpp
// Wrong
template<class T, std::size_t N> requires (sizeof(T) == N) struct C {};

// Right – put N in a separate parameter list
 template<class T, std::size_t N>
 struct C requires (sizeof(T) == N) {};
``` |
| **9** | **Misusing `auto` in a concept parameter** | `concept C = requires(auto x) { ... };` is allowed but only inside a function, not in a template parameter. | Use `auto` only in the *requires clause* of a function, not in the *concept definition*. | ```cpp
// Wrong: concept cannot take auto
concept HasX = requires(auto x) { x.x(); }; // not allowed

// Right: use a type parameter
concept HasX = requires(T x) { x.x(); };
``` |
| **10** | **Naming a concept the same as a variable, type, or function** | The keyword `concept` is a distinct namespace, but using the same name in a local scope can shadow the concept or cause confusion. | Follow the convention of naming concepts with *lowercase* letters and underscore separators (`integral`, `range_input`). | ```cpp
int integral = 0; // OK – different namespace
concept integral = requires(int x) { x + 1; }; // OK – concept in global namespace
``` |
| **11** | **Concepts that force default construction or copy/move when you don’t need it** | A concept that tests `T{}` or `std::declval<T>()` implicitly requires `T` to be default constructible. | Avoid such tests unless the property is required. Use `requires requires(T t) { ... }` instead of creating temporary objects. | ```cpp
// Too strict
concept MoveConstructible = requires(T t) { T(t); };

// Better – only check move constructor exists
concept MoveConstructible = requires(T t) { T(std::move(t)); };
``` |
| **12** | **Misusing concepts in variable templates / alias templates** | Alias templates cannot have constraints in the alias name itself. | Put the constraint on the *definition* using `requires`, or use a helper `using` with a constrained template parameter. | ```cpp
// Wrong – no place to put requires on alias
using Vec = std::vector<T> requires std::integral<T>;

// Right – helper struct
 template<class T> requires std::integral<T>
 using Vec = std::vector<T>;
``` |
| **13** | **Using concepts in CTAD (class template argument deduction) without caring about constraints** | If the CTAD rules deduce a type that does *not* satisfy the concept, you get a hard error. | Either provide deduction guides that enforce the concept, or document that the class template is only valid for types that satisfy the concept. | ```cpp
struct S {
    template<class T> requires std::integral<T>
    S(T); // CTAD deduces T
};

S s(3); // OK
S s2(3.14); // hard error – constraint not satisfied
``` |
| **14** | **Concepts that do not account for `const`/`volatile` qualifiers or references** | A concept that only tests `decltype(t + 1)` will fail on `const T` or `T&`. | Use `std::remove_cvref_t<T>` or write the constraint to be cv‑ref‑agnostic. | ```cpp
concept Addable = requires(T a, T b) { a + b; };
// Fails for const T or T&

// Fix
concept Addable = requires(std::remove_cvref_t<T> a, std::remove_cvref_t<T> b) { a + b; };
``` |
| **15** | **Compile‑time bloat from heavily constrained templates** | Each `requires` expression is evaluated at compile time; overusing them can slow down compilation dramatically. | Keep concepts lean. Factor out reusable sub‑concepts, and prefer using *traits* (like `std::is_same_v`) when you only need a boolean test. | ```cpp
// Over‑constrained
concept Complex = requires(T a, T b) { a + b; } && requires(T a) { a * a; };

// Better – split into two small concepts
concept Addable = requires(T a, T b) { a + b; };
concept Squarable = requires(T a) { a * a; };
concept Complex = Addable && Squarable;
``` |
| **16** | **Assuming a `requires` expression is *constexpr* when it isn’t** | `requires` expressions are evaluated at compile time, but the body of the expression may not be `constexpr`. | Don’t rely on `requires` to execute side‑effects; keep it pure and side‑effect‑free. | ```cpp
// Wrong – side‑effect inside requires
concept Loggable = requires(T t) { std::cout << t; };
// Causes I/O during compilation!
``` |
| **17** | **Confusing `requires` with SFINAE** | In C++20, constraints *replace* SFINAE in most cases. Using both can lead to confusing diagnostics. | Prefer concepts; if you need legacy SFINAE, keep it isolated in a helper template. | ```cpp
// Legacy SFINAE
template<class, class = void>
struct is_callable : std::false_type {};

// Modern: use concepts
concept Callable = requires { /* expression */ };
``` |
| **18** | **Not including the proper headers or using an old compiler** | Concepts are a C++20 feature; trying to compile with a pre‑C++20 compiler will silently ignore the `concept` keyword or give cryptic errors. | Ensure `-std=c++20` (or later) is enabled, and the compiler fully supports concepts (GCC ≥ 10, Clang ≥ 11, MSVC ≥ 19.28). | ```bash
# Wrong
g++ -std=c++14 file.cpp
# Correct
g++ -std=c++20 file.cpp
``` |
| **19** | **Using `requires` in a *global* context to test types** | `requires { ... }` in a non‑template context is a hard error (not SFINAE). | Put the test inside a template or a `requires` expression on a function/class. | ```cpp
// Wrong – compile‑time hard error
requires { static_assert(sizeof(int) == 4); };

// Right – wrap in a constexpr function
constexpr bool check() { requires { static_assert(sizeof(int) == 4); }; return true; }
``` |
| **20** | **Unnecessary duplication of standard library concepts** | Writing your own `SameAs`, `ConvertibleTo`, etc., can lead to subtle differences from the standard ones. | Prefer the concepts in `<concepts>` (e.g., `std::same_as`, `std::convertible_to`, `std::integral`). | ```cpp
// Avoid reinventing the wheel
concept Integral = std::integral<T>;
``` |

